Z||000000||Uaana||03-06-2000||05:02 AM||||Ok.. Sorry about this but I can't seem to find the anwser on older posts..<BR>I checked the EGR it still moves (pushed finger in and the engine wanted to stall)<BR>So that is good right? Problem is I still have glowing headers when ideling &lt;sp&gt;.<BR>So what next??? how much $$?? help.||||reg||5
Z||000001||Phil||03-06-2000||06:56 AM||||Timing, vacume leaks or EGR solenoid.||||reg||1
Z||000002||b_b_a_d||03-06-2000||01:18 PM||||I had a problem with the exhaust manifold glowing a wonderful red/orange color due to a vacuum leak from the PCV valve.  (Positive Crankcase Ventilation)  <P>Mine just wasn't set properly into the intake manifold due to a rushed reassembly :O<BR>but if memory serves me right, I believe other members have mentioned cracked PCV valves/vacuum lines causing the same problem.<P>If you have done any work on the top end, I would check this.  <P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||cobra427||03-06-2000||10:55 PM||||UANNA,you might want to check this as well. On the bottom of the throttle body,there is a pipe<BR>called an idle air pipe.It connects the throttle body via this pipe to the lower manifold. Make sure they are connected properly.The left side(as you face the engine from the trunk side)just slips into the throttle body and is sealed by an "O" ring.The other end feeds into a rubber hose that connects to the lower manifold.Make sure that this hose (1"-1.5")isn't defective.<BR>   Also , check to make sure that the EGR pipe is not cracked at the end that attaches to the plenum.It can be hard to see or feel. Recommend spraying a little WD-40 in that area and see if the idle changes.(rises) If the idle does change,remove pipe and inspect.Not a big deal.<p>[This message has been edited by cobra427 (edited 03-06-2000).]||||reg||1
